g. How To Use This AC. Pilots transitioning to unfamiliar airplanes need to develop a training strategy for mitigating the risks of operation of the new airplane. Some of these risks are inherent to any airplane that is unfamiliar to a pilot, while others are specific to the handling characteristics, performance, configurations, systems, operation, and maintenance considerations of the new airplane. (1) To develop the training strategy appropriate to the airplane the pilot is transitioning to, the pilot should first review paragraphs 1 through 7 of this AC. These paragraphs establish the basic training considerations for transitioning to any unfamiliar experimental or TC’d aircraft. Pilots should then assess the characteristics of their particular airplane in light of the families of characteristics provided in Appendix 1. For convenience, Appendix 2, Categorizing Designs or Models, provides a Reverse Lookup Table for many common experimental amateur-built airplanes. Each family of characteristics has guidance provided in Appendices 3 through 9. NOTE: An airplane may fit in more than one family. For example, a high-inertia, low-drag airplane might also employ nontraditional configuration or controls. In this case, the training strategy should reflect the considerations of both families. (2) With the aid of a certificated flight instructor (CFI), preferably with recent experience in the new make and model, the pilot should develop the training strategy as a function of the guidance provided in the appropriate appendix or appendices. These families are somewhat wide in scope and capture aircraft that display varying levels of the specific characteristics of that family. For example, a Piper Cub exhibits low-inertia/high-drag characteristics when compared to the average GA airplane available for rental, such as a Cessna C-172 or Piper PA-28. However, the Piper Cub does not display these characteristics as strongly as certain other aircraft within that family, such as the Kolb Firefly. The pilot will have to make an assessment of the criticality of the characteristics in developing a model-specific training strategy. Variances in construction may affect certain critical characteristics of the airplane. The assessment process is not complete until fully understanding the specific handling and performance characteristics of the aircraft in question. (3) In addition to the general guidance contained in paragraphs 1 through 7 and the appropriate family-specific guidance contained in Appendices 3 through 9, Appendix 10, Transitional Guidance, provides guidance for transitions to higher-performance and complex airplanes.

6. CONSIDERATIONS COMMON TO ALL AIRPLANES.

a. Hazard Identification, Risk Assessment, and Risk Mitigation. All flying comes with inherent risks. It is up to all pilots to mitigate those risks. A proven approach when transitioning into an unfamiliar airplane is the RM approach used by professional test pilots. RM is the process by which:  Hazards are identified;

 An assessment is made of the risks involved;

